{{also|dyed in the wool}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2008|April|26}}

===Etymology===
From the past participle of {{m|en|dye in the wool}}. The expression comes from the fact that [[fabric]] can be [[dyed]] in a number of ways. The [[woven]] fabric may be dyed after it is complete, or the threads may be dyed before they are woven. When a color is "dyed in the wool," the wool itself is dyed before being spun into [[thread]]s, so the colour is least likely to fade or change. (''Dyes: Webster’s Quotations, Facts and Phrases''. Icon Group International. 2008, [http://books.google.com/books?id=wnmeiwmIv9YC&pg=PA344 p. 344.]).

