# {{lb|en|of|textiles}} Having the [[fibre]]s dyed before they are formed into [[cloth]].
#* '''1747''', John Smith, ''Chronicon Rusticum-Commerciale; or, Memoirs of Wool, &c.,'' London: T. Osborne, Volume{{nbsp}}2, Chapter{{nbsp}}156, p.{{nbsp}}431,<sup>[https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015024834155&view=1up&seq=447&skin=2021&q1=%22dyed%20in%20the%20wool%22]</sup>
#*: {{...}} some [[drugget|Druggets]] with Thread, that are dyed in the Piece; the others are '''dyed in the Wool''', ''i.{{nbsp}}e.'' the Wool of which they are made, is dyed of several Colours, before it is carded, spun, and weaved.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1810|title=Redeeming the Time|journal={{w|The Evangelical Magazine}}|volume=18|month=January|titleurl=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=hvd.ah6lr7&view=1up&seq=17&skin=2021&q1=%22dyed%20in%20the%20wool%22|page=7
|passage=That cloth will keep its colour best that is '''dyed in the wool'''; and the vessel will longest retain the scent of that liquor with which it is first seasoned.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|figuratively|of a person}} Firmly [[establish]]ed in one's [[belief]]s or [[habit]]s; having a specified [[characteristic]], [[identity]], etc. deeply [[ingrain]]ed in one's [[nature]].
#: {{syn|en|inveterate}}
#: {{ux|en|Smith was a '''dyed-in-the-wool''' typist and never really got used to writing on computers.}}
#: {{ux|en|John Major was described by his opponents as a '''dyed-in-the-wool''' Conservative.}}
#* '''1820''', {{w|Daniel Webster}}, ''A Discourse Delivered at Plymouth, December{{nbsp}}22, 1820,'' Boston: Wells and Lilly, 1830, p.{{nbsp}}101,<sup>[https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=hvd.hnyvnw&view=1up&seq=303&skin=2021&q1=%22dyed%20in%20the%20wool%22]</sup>
#*: We all know a process, sir, by which the whole {{w|Essex Junto}} could, in one hour, be all washed white from their ancient federalism, and come out, every one of them, an original democrat, '''dyed in the wool'''!
#* {{RQ:Hawthorne Scarlet Letter|Introductory|page=[https://archive.org/details/scarletletterrom01hawt/page/18/mode/1up?q=%22dyed+in+the+wool%22 18]|text=He might truly be termed a legitimate son of the revenue system, '''dyed in the wool''', or rather, born in the purple; since his sire, a Revolutionary colonel, and formerly collector of the port, had created an office for him, and appointed him to fill it, at a period of the early ages which few living men can now remember.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1944|month=November and December|author=Talisman|title=A Broadening Horizon|journal=Railway Magazine|page=340|text=Although appreciating the rapidity and frequency of the Southern electric services I was now to use on short journeys, I became more than ever convinced that electric traction offers very little of interest to the '''dyed-in-the-wool''' railwayist.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1956|author={{w|Rose Macaulay}}|title={{w|The Towers of Trebizond}}|location=London|publisher=Collins|chapter=23|url=https://www.fadedpage.com/books/20210280/html.php
|passage=For all he says he isn’t, he’s a bit of an [[ultramontane]], in practice though not in theory, and we can’t have that in the Church of England, we must stay '''dyed-in-the-wool''' Anglican.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author={{w|Philip Roth}}|title={{w|The Plot Against America}}|location=London|publisher=Jonathan Cape|chapter=8|page=304|url=https://archive.org/details/plotagainstameri0000roth/page/304/mode/1up?q=%22dyed-in-the-wool%22
|passage=Our president is no lover of Jews and more than likely a '''dyed-in-the-wool''' anti-Semite {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 April 7|author=Christian Wolmar|title=Electrification is a given... but comfort matters as well|journal=RAIL|issue=928|page=47|text=Again, to go back to the history of British Railways, there were moves to introduce electrification more widely when the West Coast Main Line was sparked up in the 1960s, but this was rejected by '''dyed-in-the-wool''' old regional railway managers who did not like the hassle of putting up the wires.}}

